## Chapter 8: Stakeholder Communication and Lifecycle Management — Flashcards

| # | Front (Question) | Back (Answer) |
|---|-----------------|---------------|
| 1 | Standard ADR (Architecture Decision Record) section order? | Title/Status → Context → Decision → Alternatives Considered (with rationale) → Consequences. Exam tip: alternatives + rationale must come before consequences to defend the decision. |
| 2 | Canary release vs. Blue-green deployment — key difference? | Canary = incremental traffic % shift with monitoring gates (e.g., 5% → 25% → 100%). Blue-green = instant full switch between two complete environments. Exam tip: canary is for gradual risk reduction; blue-green is for fast, reversible full cutover. |
| 3 | Shadow traffic testing (dark launch) — what makes it zero-risk to users? | Live requests are duplicated to the new version for comparison/logging, but its output is never returned to the user. Exam tip: use this before any live canary when you need pure offline quality comparison. |
| 4 | Semantic versioning rule for an additive, non-breaking prompt/agent change? | Bump MINOR version (e.g., v2.3.1 → v2.4.0). MAJOR = breaking change, PATCH = bug fix only. Exam tip: adding a new optional field is always a minor bump, never major. |
| 5 | Hard cutover vs. backward-compatibility shim — tradeoff? | Hard cutover: fast, simple, but forces all consumers to migrate at once (high business risk). Shim/adapter: slower, more engineering effort, but preserves legacy integrations during transition (lower business risk). Exam tip: choose shim when external/enterprise consumers can't migrate on your timeline. |
| 6 | How to translate a model-upgrade cost/latency tradeoff for a non-technical stakeholder? | Map the tradeoff onto a KPI they already track (cost per resolved ticket, latency per user-facing request) instead of raw benchmark scores. Exam tip: never lead with model benchmark numbers (MMLU, GPQA) for business audiences. |
| 7 | What triangle of tradeoffs must every model-migration decision memo address? | Cost, latency, and quality — improving one typically pressures the other two. Exam tip: scenario questions test whether you pick the option that best balances all three for the stated business constraint, not the one that maximizes a single axis. |
| 8 | What should trigger an automatic rollback during a phased rollout? | A predefined quality/error-rate/latency threshold breach monitored in real time against a baseline — not a manual, ad-hoc decision after user complaints. Exam tip: rollback criteria must be defined BEFORE rollout starts, in the change-management plan. |
| 9 | Why is a single pre-launch acceptance test insufficient for lifecycle management? | It can't detect post-launch quality drift caused by changing usage patterns or data distribution shifts over time. Exam tip: continuous monitoring with alerting on drift is required, not a one-time gate. |
| 10 | A/B testing vs. canary release — key distinction? | A/B testing compares two variants to measure a business outcome (often user-visible, statistically evaluated). Canary release is a deployment risk-mitigation technique that gradually shifts traffic to a new version based on operational health metrics. Exam tip: they can overlap but serve different primary goals (experimentation vs. safe rollout). |
| 11 | What belongs in a deprecation/change-management communication to stakeholders? | Timeline, affected consumers/integrations, migration path (shim vs. hard cutover), rollback plan, and the business impact if no action is taken. Exam tip: a deprecation notice without a migration path and timeline fails the 'change-management planning' objective. |
| 12 | Who should approve a production prompt/agent change before it ships? | A defined change-management gate — typically requiring sign-off from both the technical owner and the business/product stakeholder affected by behavior changes. Exam tip: scenario questions testing 'who approves' favor the option with explicit review gates over informal or engineering-only approval. |
